            <ArticleTitle>Extraosseous Osteosarcoma of the Liver Demonstrated on 18F-FDG PET/CT Imaging.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>650-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/RLU.0000000000001210</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 66-year-old woman presented with abdominal pain for 1 month. Ultrasonography displayed multiple hepatic masses that were thought as metastases. FDG PET/CT was performed to assess the nature of these masses and to search primary malignancy. The images showed elevated FDG activity in the partially calcified hypodense lesions in the liver without abnormality elsewhere. The lesions were subsequently confirmed as primary extraosseous osteosarcoma in the liver. The patient received liver transplantation. Six months later, her CA-125 was significantly increased. The follow-up PET/CT scan demonstrated the widespread metastases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
